Core rule
If a Hugging Face model uses one of those architectures, it will usually run.
vLLM loads a model if its config.json → "architectures" matches an implemented backend.
So you don’t check “is my exact model listed?”
You check “does my model use a supported architecture?”

vLLM can run embedding models that use supported HF architectures.

Even if a model is not natively implemented, it may still run via the Transformers backend.

How to test support (the only test that never lies)
from vllm import LLM
llm = LLM(model="your/model")


Anything that is:
causal LM
HF-compatible
not doing weird custom attention
→ has a high probability of working.


1) The first gate: architecture (vLLM compatibility)
🔍 Field:
"architectures": [...]
This must map to something vLLM implements.

Examples
✅ Good for vLLM:
"architectures": ["Qwen2VLForConditionalGeneration"]
"architectures": ["LlamaForCausalLM"]
"architectures": ["MistralForCausalLM"]

⚠️ Risky / slower fallback:
"architectures": ["SomeCustomModel"]

This decides native kernels vs Transformers fallback → 2–10× speed difference.

🧠 2) Context length (KV-cache = your real VRAM killer)
🔍 Field:
"max_position_embeddings"

This number is not decorative.

Example:
32768 → manageable
131072 → your KV cache explodes

KV cache memory grows with:
context × layers × hidden_size
So two 7B models with different context lengths can have wildly different memory needs.

🧠 3) Hidden size (the true “model width”)
🔍 Field:
"hidden_size"

This controls:
KV-cache size
activation memory
throughput

Example comparison:
Model	hidden_size
7B slim	4096
7B wide	8192

Same “7B”, completely different runtime behaviour.


🧠 4) Number of layers (latency multiplier)
🔍 Field:
"num_hidden_layers"

More layers =
slower tokens/sec
more KV cache

Rough mental model:
latency ∝ layers

🧠 5) Attention heads & GQA (speed magic)
🔍 Fields:
"num_attention_heads"
"num_key_value_heads"

If:
num_key_value_heads < num_attention_heads

you have GQA / MQA

That means:
✅ smaller KV cache
✅ faster inference
✅ better for long context
This is why modern Llama/Qwen feel efficient.

🧠 6) Rope scaling / long-context tricks
🔍 Field:
"rope_scaling"

Tells you:
native long context
YaRN / dynamic NTK / linear scaling

This affects:
quality at long context
whether the “128k” is real or marketing

🧠 7) Vision-language specific fields
Look for a vision_config block:
"vision_config": {
  "image_size": 448,
  "patch_size": 14,
  "hidden_size": ...
}

Why it matters:
Image tokens roughly:
(image_size / patch_size)²

So:
Resolution	Visual tokens
224	256
448	1024
896	4096 🚨

🧠 8) Dtype (does it expect BF16?)
🔍 Field:
"torch_dtype": "bfloat16"

If your GPU:
supports BF16 → perfect
doesn’t → conversion overhead

For A40/A100 → you’re fine.

🧠 9) Quantisation hint (sometimes present)
"quantization_config"

Tells you if the model is:
AWQ
GPTQ
FP8

This affects:
whether vLLM can load it directly
which kernels get used

🧠 10) Vocabulary size (tiny but real VRAM cost)
🔍 Field:
"vocab_size"

Large vocab = larger embedding matrix.
Not huge, but relevant in tight fits.


🧠 Example: why two “7B” models behave differently
Model A:
hidden_size: 4096
layers: 32
context: 32k
GQA: yes


Model B:
hidden_size: 8192
layers: 40
context: 128k
no GQA

Both marketed as 7B.
Runtime reality:
Model B will feel like a 20B.

Most people compare:
benchmarks vs benchmarks

System builders compare:
config.json vs GPU topology

That’s how you pick the model that actually survives production.


Both A40 and A100 will run almost any vLLM-compatible model, so the question is no longer “does it run?” but:
How fast, how large, how many concurrent requests, and with what context length?
This is where GPU architecture actually matters in a meaningful, measurable way.

🧠 The core philosophical truth

For LLM/VLM inference:
VRAM = survival
Memory bandwidth = speed
Tensor core generation = efficiency at scale

Both cards support BF16/FP16 and modern kernels → compatibility is not the problem.

⚙️ A40 vs A100 — the raw reality
Feature	A40	A100 80GB
VRAM	48 GB	80 GB
Memory bandwidth	~696 GB/s	~2039 GB/s
Compute capability	8.6	8.0
Tensor throughput	lower	much higher
NVLink	❌	✅ (multi-GPU scaling)

🚀 What this means for vLLM in practice
1️⃣ Model size ceiling
A40 (48 GB)
Comfort zone:
7B–14B FP16
32B AWQ
72B AWQ (tight but doable with tuning)

A100 80 GB
New universe unlocked:
70B FP16 → easy
72B FP16 → easy
large VLMs with batching
massive KV cache

This is the single biggest difference.

2️⃣ Throughput (tokens/sec)
A100 wins hard because:
3× memory bandwidth
more tensor throughput

LLMs are memory-bound → bandwidth dominates.

Typical real-world gain:
A100 ≈ 2–3× A40 tokens/sec
Same model. Same quantisation.

3️⃣ Long context performance
KV cache lives in VRAM.

So:
A40 → good for 32k / moderate batching
A100 → 128k context + batching becomes practical
For your document pipelines, that’s enormous.

4️⃣ Concurrency (vLLM’s superpower)
vLLM shines when you batch many requests.
A100 can:
hold larger KV cache
serve more users simultaneously
This is why it feels “production-grade”.

5️⃣ Vision-language workloads
VLMs eat memory via image tokens.
A100 allows:
higher image resolution
multi-image prompts
real batching
A40 forces trade-offs.


🧠 So when you open a Hugging Face model, what tells you which GPU you need?
You look at config and ask:
Question 1
How big are the weights?

→ decides if A40 is enough or A100 required

Question 2
How long is the context?

From:
max_position_embeddings

Huge number → A100 becomes attractive.

Question 3
For VLMs — how many visual tokens?
From:
vision_config.image_size
patch_size

High resolution → A100 territory if you want batching.

🧪 A concrete intuition using your typical models
Qwen3-30B-AWQ
A40 → runs well
A100 → runs fast + large batch

Qwen3-72B FP16
A40 → ❌
A100 → ✅ comfortable

Qwen2.5-VL-7B
Single image:
A40 → fine
A100 → batch many images

Multi-image reasoning:
A40 → constrained
A100 → smooth


🧠 The deep architectural insight
Both are Ampere.
So:
❌ “Will kernels work?” → yes on both
✅ “How much parallelism can I sustain?” → A100 wins

This is not compatibility — it’s throughput economics.


🧠 The meta-rule you can carry forever
When choosing between A40 and A100:
Ask:
Is this a “fit” problem or a “speed / scale” problem?

If it fits on A40 → use A40 (cost efficiency).
If you need:
bigger model
longer context
higher concurrency

→ A100.


